If you're building a web app with live Markdown preview—like a note-taking app or CMS—you need client-side conversion.

**marked.js** is the most popular option:

```javascript
import { marked } from 'marked';

const html = marked.parse('# Hello World\n\nThis is **bold** text.');
```

**Showdown** is another solid choice with extensive configuration:

```javascript
import showdown from 'showdown';
const converter = new showdown.Converter();
const html = converter.makeHtml(markdownText);
```

Both libraries support GitHub-flavored Markdown through options or extensions. They also allow custom renderers to modify how specific elements are generated—useful for adding CSS classes, lazy-loading images, or sanitizing output to prevent XSS.

For React apps, **react-markdown** wraps these parsers in a component that renders Markdown as React elements instead of an HTML string—safer and more flexible.
